On Wed, Jan 28, 2015 at 10:16 AM, Gleki Arxokuna <gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com
> wrote:

>
> How can {lo se xi vei pa a re no'a} be monoparsed in pragmatic terms? It
> still has two readings.
>

It has a single parse. One that involves a disjunction.


> {mi prami do} still has zillions of readings including {mi ba'o prami do}
> and {mi ba zu ze'i ru'i prami do}.
>

Not really, it only expresses the relation "prami" between two arguments
"mi" and "do", basically P{m,d) in FOPL notation. There's no syntactic
ambiguity. The wikipedia article on ambiguity discusses the distinction
between ambiguity and vagueness, as well as different types of ambiguity:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambiguity

mu'o mi'e xorxes

